+## Overview
+Brick-based structures replace the variable-resolution hierarchical subdivision of sparse voxel octrees with fixed-size cubic blocks ("bricks") to optimize for hardware-friendly memory access and parallel construction [S1, unofficial]. They solve the traversal complexity and shader divergence of recursive tree structures by enabling branch-free, linear memory access patterns suitable for real-time ray tracing and GPU cache coherency [G].
+## Shared principles
+The family partitions the 3D domain into fixed-granularity cubic blocks rather than recursive nodes [S1, unofficial]. These blocks are stored in a sparse container (e.g., a low-resolution grid or hash map) that maps spatial regions to brick data [G]. This approach prioritizes uniform granularity to balance memory usage and traversal speed, specifically optimizing for hardware interpolation and parallel processing [S1, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
 
-    ### [Sparse Voxel Brick Set](11886-sparse-voxel-brick-set.md)
-    A sparse volume representation that groups Signed Distance Field (SDF) data into fixed-size cubic blocks, each associated with a raytracing Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) [Sparse Voxel Brick Set](11886-sparse-voxel-brick-set.md). It balances memory efficiency and real-time constructibility, specifically optimizing for GPU cache coherency and parallel construction [Sparse Voxel Brick Set](11886-sparse-voxel-brick-set.md). Unlike Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) which vary resolution, or Grid Sphere Tracing which stores every voxel, the Brick Set uses a uniform brick granularity to balance memory usage and traversal speed, making it particularly effective in complex scenes [Sparse Voxel Brick Set](11886-sparse-voxel-brick-set.md). It builds on [Octree-based structures](2792-octree-based-structures.md) by replacing hierarchical subdivision with fixed-granularity blocks for hardware-friendly memory access.
+### [Sparse Voxel Brick Set](11886-sparse-voxel-brick-set.md)
+Groups Signed Distance Field (SDF) data into fixed-size cubic blocks, each associated with a raytracing Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB). It balances memory efficiency and real-time constructibility, specifically optimizing for GPU cache coherency and parallel construction. Unlike Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) which vary resolution, or Grid Sphere Tracing which stores every voxel, the Brick Set uses a uniform brick granularity to balance memory usage and traversal speed, making it particularly effective in complex scenes. Choose it when hardware-friendly memory access and parallel construction are prioritized over variable resolution.
 
-    ### [Brick Map](21177-brick-map.md)
-    A two-level sparse spatial data structure for ray tracing that combines a low-resolution full voxel grid with high-resolution sparse 'bricks' to store triangle indices, avoiding the traversal complexity of BVHs and SVOs while skipping free space [Brick Map](21177-brick-map.md). It is designed specifically for real-time ray tracing of secondary rays (reflections/refractions) starting from surfaces, supporting dynamic content and allowing ray bouncing with controlled execution time [Brick Map](21177-brick-map.md). Unlike SVOs which require stack-based or recursive traversal, the Brick Map allows hard-coded, branch-free traversal by always reading the low-resolution cell to determine if a brick is present, resulting in low shader divergence [Brick Map](21177-brick-map.md).
+### [Brick Map](21177-brick-map.md)
+A two-level sparse spatial data structure for ray tracing that combines a low-resolution full voxel grid with high-resolution sparse 'bricks' to store triangle indices, avoiding the traversal complexity of BVHs and SVOs while skipping free space. It is designed specifically for real-time ray tracing of secondary rays (reflections/refractions) starting from surfaces, supporting dynamic content and allowing ray bouncing with controlled execution time. Unlike SVOs which require stack-based or recursive traversal, the Brick Map allows hard-coded, branch-free traversal by always reading the low-resolution cell to determine if a brick is present, resulting in low shader divergence. Choose it for real-time secondary ray tracing with dynamic content where branch-free traversal is critical.
 
-    ### Sparse Voxel Octree with 3x3 Bricks
-    Uses 3x3 voxels per octree node corner for hardware interpolation [S1, unofficial]. Memory usage is less than half that of previous solutions [S1, unofficial].
+### Sparse Voxel Octree with 3x3 Bricks
+Uses 3x3 voxels per octree node corner for hardware interpolation [S1, unofficial]. Memory usage is less than half that of previous solutions [S1, unofficial].
 
-    ## Background
-    The concept of using small bricks within an octree structure for real-time indirect illumination was introduced by Crassin et al. in 2009 [S1, unofficial]. This approach was designed to avoid costly precomputation steps and allow dynamic updates for animated objects and dynamic modifications of the environment [S1, unofficial]. The technique utilizes hardware interpolation while significantly reducing memory usage compared to previous solutions [S1, unofficial].
